so according to the biden administration  the war in afghanistan is over but what  legally allowed that war to happen in  the first place and could it happen  again only congress has the power to  declare war it's right there in article  1 of the constitution so how is it that  presidents basically seem to have  unilateral ability to declare war  whenever they want in this particular  case we're talking about the  authorization for use of military force  or aumf the 2001 aomf allowed the  president to go to war in afghanistan  and there were also aomfs in 1991 and  2002 that allowed the president to go to  war in iraq those are still on the books  which leads many to say that perhaps now  we should remove them since those wars  are over and we don't want presidents  extending those aumfs to go to war in  other places this is part of a  long-standing battle between congress  and the president over who can declare  and continue to go to war it's  absolutely not a settled constitutional  question but the ending of the war in  afghanistan has raised these questions  once again so perhaps this time maybe  something will be done about it
 

